n the DAG. Correct per Rule 10.\n76\t- license-validation (E008) → repo-onboarding (E006/E007): no edge. → konflux-onboarding\n77\t  (E009/E010): no edge. Correct per Rules 7/8.\n78\t- E008 → E011 (license → general implementation) is present and valid per Rule 9.\n79\t\n80\tThe RELATED_IMAGE digest coupling between the Konflux epics and E012/E014 is correctly\n81\tmodeled as AC gates (\"SHA256-pinned digests available at packaging time — a configuration\n82\treference, not a build dependency\"), not DAG edges — exactly the Rule 13 configuration-\n83\treference treatment. E006 → E009 and E006 → E011 (repo must exist before the pipeline\n84\tbuilds from it and before manifests are pinned) are genuine artifact dependencies.\n85\tE011 → E013 is justified: the feature flag and the BFF's AgentRuntime listing consume the\n86\tDSC schema extension and deployed component E011 produces. Critical path length 5 (E006 →\n87\tE011 → E012 → E014 → E015) is within the L-strategy heuristic (5-7). No conditional\n88\tbranch files exist, so no branch-file checks apply. Cross-checking epic content for\n89\tuncaptured data dependencies found none beyond the AC-gated configuration references.\n90\t\n91\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n92\t\n93\tEvery epic carries a single component/team tuple matching the strategy's component\n94\tmappings (canonical names verified against .context/rhai-components.txt: Agentic, AI Core\n95\tPlatform, AI Core Dashboard, QE, Documentation). The two repo-onboarding epics (E006,\n96\tE007) are correctly separate: different repos, each with its own fork/sync work — not\n97\tscope duplication. E009/E010 split the two image families rather than bundling them,\n98\twhich also keeps each Konflux epic near the strategy's 1-1.5 sprint per-image estimate.\n99\tNo epic mixes code implementation with content authoring (E015 is pure docs-authoring;\n100\tE014 is pure QE). No two epics build the same surface: the dashboard work is entirely in\n101\tE013, the CSV work entirely in E012, the controller work entirely in E011.\n102\t\n103\tMinor (not scored): E011 spans the full component-controller stream the strategy\n104\testimates at 2-3 sprints (CRD definition, DSC schema extension, controller + registry,\n105\tkustomize integration, GC logic — with lifecycle testing already split out into E014).\n106\tThat is at or slightly above the ~2-week epic guideline, though it is one precedented\n107\tpattern in one repo/team and splitting it (e.g., CRD schema vs controller) would create\n108\tartificial boundaries.\n109\t\n110\t### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n111\t\n112\tAll five Investigations (E001-E005) resolve genuine strategy uncertainties that change\n113\tdownstream scope or existence, each with a dependent epic: E001→E011, E002→E010,\n114\tE003→E013, E004→E011, E005→E011. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts.\n115\tEvery epic with `gated_by` set (E010, E011, E013) has a populated `gate_failure_impact`\n116\twith a real scope change on failure, and every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's\n117\tdirect `dependencies`.\n118\t\n119\tOne gating-metadata gap: E011 lists three Investigation dependencies (E001, E004, E005)\n120\tbut the frontmatter `gated_by` is single-valued and records only the primary gate (E001).\n121\tThe E004 (cleanup semantics) and E005 (OTEL scope) gates are documented in the summary's\n122\tDAG justification and Health Warnings, and E005's outcome is folded into E011's\n123\t`gate_failure_impact` text — but E004's outcome is not mentioned there, and automated\n124\tconsumers reading frontmatter alone will detect only one of E011's three gates. This is\n125\tthe canonical level-1 condition (Investigation dependency missing `gated_by` on a\n126\tdownstream epic); the schema cannot express multi-gates, and the decomposition mitigates\n127\tit by documenting the other gates, so it costs a point rather than zeroing the criterion.\n128\t\n129\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n130\t\n131\tTyp